                  "content": "SELECTED: candidate_279\n\nCONFIDENCE: HIGH\n\nEXPLANATION:\nThe fix commit changes this line in Minecraft.java:\n\n    if (currentScreen == null) {\n        if (Keyboard.getEventKey() == Keyboard.KEY_ESCAPE) {\n-           if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel){\n+           if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel || !isOnline()){\n                pause();\n            } else {\n                Mouse.setGrabbed(false);\n            }\n\ni.e. it adds \"|| !isOnline()\" to the condition that decides whether pressing\nEscape should open the pause menu. Without that clause, if\npacketHandler.isLoadingLevel happened to be true while playing singleplayer\n(isOnline() == false), pressing Escape would just call\nMouse.setGrabbed(false) instead of pause(), so the pause/menu screen would\nnever show up in singleplayer \u2014 exactly the bug described in the fix commit\nmessage (\"Fixed a bug which was causing the menu to not show up in\nsingleplayer\").\n\ncandidate_279 is the commit that first introduced this exact\nif/else structure:\n\n    if (currentScreen == null) {\n        if (Keyboard.getEventKey() == Keyboard.KEY_ESCAPE) {\n+           if (!packetHandler.isLoadingLevel){\n+               pause();\n+           } else {\n+               Mouse.setGrabbed(false);\n+           }\n        }\n\nBefore candidate_279, this block simply called pause() unconditionally on\nEscape, so the missing-menu bug could not occur. candidate_279 is therefore\nthe first commit that made pause() conditional on packetHandler.isLoadingLevel\nwithout accounting for singleplayer (isOnline() == false), which is precisely\nthe root cause fixed later by adding \"|| !isOnline()\". candidate_280 only\ntouches an unrelated Escape-handling block earlier in the file (toggling\nbetween pause()/Mouse.setGrabbed(false) in a different loading-screen input\nloop) and does not affect this bug. candidate_281 is the fix commit itself.\n"
                }
              }
            ]
